msgid "Core Features"
msgstr ""

msgid "Basic Building Blocks"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The dynamic parts of a template include comments, expressions, sections and "
"unparsed character data."
msgstr ""

msgid "Comments"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A comment starts with the sequence `{!` and ends with the sequence `!}`, e.g."
" `{! This is a comment !}`.\n"
"Can be multiline and may contain expressions and sections: `{! {#if true} "
"!}`.\n"
"The content of a comment is completely ignored when rendering the output."
msgstr ""

msgid "Expressions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An <<expressions,expression>> outputs an evaluated value.\n"
"It consists of one or more parts.\n"
"A part may represent simple properties: `{foo}`, `{item.name}`, and virtual "
"methods: `{item.get(name)}`, `{name ?: 'John'}`.\n"
"An expression may also start with a namespace: `{inject:colors}`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sections"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A <<sections,section>> may contain static text, expressions and nested "
"sections: `{#if foo.active}{foo.name}{/if}`.\n"
"The name in the closing tag is optional: `{#if active}ACTIVE!{/}`.\n"
"A section can be empty: `{#myTag image=true /}`.\n"
"Some sections support optional end tags, i.e. if the end tag is missing then "
"the section ends where the parent section ends.\n"
"A section may also declare nested section blocks: `{#if item.valid} Valid. "
"{#else} Invalid. {/if}` and decide which block to render."
msgstr ""

msgid "Unparsed Character Data"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It is used to mark the content that should be rendered but _not parsed_.\n"
"It starts with the sequence `\\{|`  and ends with the sequence `|\\}`: `\\{| "
"<script>if(true){alert('Qute is cute!')};</script> |\\}`, and could be multi-"
"line."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The number of `|` (pipe) characters in the opening delimiter can be "
"increased to avoid conflicts with content that itself contains `|\\}`.\n"
"The closing delimiter must use the same number of pipes as the opening "
"delimiter.\n"
"For example, `\\{||||` starts an unparsed block that only ends with "
"`||||\\}`, making it safe to include `|\\}` in the content: `\\{|||| content "
"with |\\} inside ||||\\}`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Consecutive `|` characters immediately after `\\{|` are now counted as part "
"of the opening delimiter.\n"
"For example, `\\{||foo|\\}` was previously interpreted as single-pipe "
"unparsed character data with content `|foo`, but is now interpreted as a "
"double-pipe opening that requires `||\\}` to close."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Previously, unparsed character data could start with `{[` and end with `]}`. "
"This syntax is now removed due to common collisions with constructs from "
"other languages."
msgstr ""

msgid "Identifiers and Tags"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Identifiers are used in expressions and section tags.\n"
"A valid identifier is a sequence of non-whitespace characters.\n"
"However, users are encouraged to only use valid Java identifiers in "
"expressions."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When parsing a template document the parser identifies all _tags_.\n"
"A tag starts and ends with a curly bracket, e.g. `{foo}`.\n"
"The content of a tag must start with:"
msgstr ""

msgid "a digit, or"
msgstr ""

msgid "an alphabet character, or"
msgstr ""

msgid "underscore, or"
msgstr ""

msgid "a built-in command: `#`, `!`, `@`, `/`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If it does not start with any of the above it is ignored by the parser."
msgstr ""

msgid "Parsed: an expression that starts with underscore."
msgstr ""

msgid "Parsed: a comment"
msgstr ""

msgid "Ignored: starts with whitespace."
msgstr ""

msgid "Ignored: starts with `{`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Ignored: starts with `\"`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Removing Standalone Lines From the Template"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, the parser removes standalone lines from the template output.\n"
"A *standalone line* is a line that contains at least one section tag (e.g. "
"`{#each}` and `{/each}`), parameter declaration (e.g. `{@org.acme.Foo foo}`) "
"or comment but no expression and no non-whitespace character.\n"
"In other words, a line that contains no section tag or a parameter "
"declaration is *not* a standalone line.\n"
"Likewise, a line that contains an _expression_ or a _non-whitespace "
"character_ is *not* a standalone line."
msgstr ""

msgid "This is a standalone line and will be removed."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Not a standalone line - contains an expression and non-whitespace characters"
msgstr ""

msgid "Not a standalone line - contains no section tag/parameter declaration"
msgstr ""

msgid "This is a standalone line."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An expression is evaluated and outputs the value.\n"
"It has one or more parts, where each part represents either a property "
"accessor (aka Field Access Expression) or a virtual method invocation (aka "
"Method Invocation Expression)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When accessing the properties you can either use the dot notation or bracket "
"notation.\n"
"In the `object.property` (dot notation) syntax, the `property` must be a "
"<<identifiers,valid identifier>>.\n"
"In the `object[property_name]` (bracket notation) syntax, the "
"`property_name` has to be a non-null <<literals,literal>> value."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An expression can start with an optional namespace followed by a colon (`:`)."
"\n"
"A valid namespace consists of alphanumeric characters and underscores.\n"
"Namespace expressions are resolved differently - see also "
"<<expression_resolution>>."
msgstr ""

msgid "no namespace, one part: `name`"
msgstr ""

msgid "no namespace, two parts: `item`, `name`"
msgstr ""

msgid "equivalent to `{item.name}` but using the bracket notation"
msgstr ""

msgid "namespace `global`, one part: `colors`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A part of an expression can be a _virtual method_ in which case the name can "
"be followed by a list of comma-separated parameters in parentheses.\n"
"A parameter of a virtual method can be either a nested expression or a "
"<<literals,literal>> value.\n"
"We call these methods _\"virtual\"_ because they do not have to be backed by "
"a real Java method.\n"
"You can learn more about virtual methods in the <<virtual_methods,following "
"section>>."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"no namespace, two parts - `item`, `getLabels(1)`, the second part is a "
"virtual method with name `getLabels` and params `1`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"infix notation that can be used for virtual methods with single parameter, "
"translated to `name.or('John')`; no namespace, two parts - `name`, "
"`or('John')`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An expression can also use a <<literals,literal>> value as the base, "
"followed by property accessors or virtual method invocations."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"string literal as the base followed by a property accessor; outputs `FOO`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"integer literal as the base followed by a property accessor; outputs `1`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"in a <<let_section,let>> section parameter, a literal base can be used "
"directly inside parentheses"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"a literal base can also be used in nested expressions passed as parameters "
"of virtual methods"
msgstr ""

